+report = '''# Part D report
+
+D.1–D.11 are written in D.tex, D.12–D.16 in D_nonlinear.tex, and D.17–D.23 in D_surface.tex. The combined register contains 17 Proved, 5 Refuted and 1 Known statement, with no obsolete compound Open row. The Known smooth-model statement includes a full derivation from the exact source theorem. The package independently rechecks the LM1 divided-power and positive-power arguments rather than treating the earlier review as authority. Both supplements have separate result, coverage, reading and source ledgers.
+
+Decisive corrections are the primitive-lattice counterexample k ↦ 2k, the failure of the proposed generators/relations even for Ga² (the characteristic-p Heisenberg test), and the fact that scan 231 is a modular-cover construction whose actual presentations occur on scans 234–237.
+
+Both named nonlinear tests are settled positively. Over Z/4 the proof uses the square-zero identity kernel. Over characteristic-zero dual numbers, D.11 constructs Weil restriction without a flatness assumption, computes its reduction kernel as a vector group, restricts to the actual algebraic image, and proves that the inverse image of the cyclic unipotent closure is unipotent. Exponential and adjunction give the unique additive one-parameter subgroup through the section. The nonflat group Spec A[t]/(epsilon t) checks why surjectivity onto the whole special fibre must not be assumed.
+
+The two computational levels are explicit rational Malcev classes 1 and 2 with a specified integral truncated-Magnus representation. They are not claimed to be canonical first and second levels of §52. The torus twist requires dilation by 2 on the second Magnus lattice, but is integral at 3. The four-puncture separating twist has an integral square-zero logarithm. d_checks.py verifies exact words and independent matrices through nilpotent class 4; all outputs are rational or integral, with no numerical tolerance.
+
+The original unrestricted quasi-compact-base assertion is refuted in D.14 by a smooth group over a one-dimensional non-quasi-separated doubled base; multiplication by an elliptic curve makes the group nonaffine. The base is not affine, so this does not refute all ring-base versions. Original scans 10–12 confirm that separatedness modifies G and that S is only assumed quasi-compact before the argument restricts to Noetherian integral bases.
+
+D.12 proves that local parameters glue after a common dilation on quasi-compact overlaps. D.13 settles all quasi-compact bases killed by one nonzero integer. D.15 settles characteristic-zero Dedekind bases and explicit finite free and nonflat nilpotent thickenings. D.16 supplies the integer-localization lemma: a parameter over R[1/n] extends after n-power dilation, using an affine identity chart and a denominator F congruent to 1 modulo n. Finite-presentation descent then settles every integral Noetherian base whose rational fibre is regular of dimension at most one, including all DVRs, all Dedekind bases and Spec Z[t], with no affineness or flatness of G. Standard finite free nilpotent thickenings follow as well.
+
